  "answer": "If the absolute offset between instrument time and the observatory reference **exceeds two seconds**, the gateway must pause schedule execution, notify the duty operator, and leave queued reservations unchanged. It may resume only after **two consecutive reference checks** each report an offset within two seconds. [clock.md:3–7](clock.md#L3-L7)\n\nIf the reference clock is unavailable, execution must remain paused: a local clock reading alone cannot satisfy either recovery check. [clock.md:9–11](clock.md#L9-L11)\n\nThe gateway must not use the retired prototype’s recovery behavior of clearing pending reservations or restarting on the first local clock sample. A planner may compute a plan while execution is suspended, but that plan does not bypass the clock check or a weather interlock. [architecture.md:5–11](architecture.md#L5-L11)",
  "answer_quality": "NOT_GRADED",
  "source_facts_quality": "NOT_GRADED",
  "timing_boundary": "wall-v2: Python time.time_ns versus JS Date.now, same-host epoch clock; JS millisecond resolution, wall-clock steps not excluded. Monotonic clocks are process-local only. Legacy Python wall time is never reconstructed. Not remote arrival."
